# The canonical comparison `ℱ^∧ ⟶ (lim ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]` and its projections

Let `X` be a locally Noetherian scheme, `𝓘 ⊂ 𝒪_X` a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals and `ℱ` a
coherent `𝒪_X`-module. `Completion/AdicQuotientLimitComplete.lean` proves that there is exactly
one map

`c : ℱ^∧ ⟶ (lim ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]`

compatible with the completion unit and the limit lift. This file *names* that map, names the
canonical maps `ℱ^∧ ⟶ (ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]` onto the terms of the tower, and records how the three fit
together: the projections of the tower form a cone, and composing `c` with the `n`-th limit
projection gives the `n`-th canonical map. It also upgrades part (4) of [Stacks, Tag 0A0K] to the
form that *pins its projections*, which is what any argument comparing two derived-limit
presentations of the same tower needs.

Nothing here assumes quasi-compactness of `X` or that `𝓘` is generated by global sections.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Algebraization.completionToAdicQuotient` — the canonical map `ℱ^∧ ⟶ (ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]`,
  which exists because each term of the tower is derived complete along `𝓘`
  (`Stacks.Algebraization.isDerivedComplete_single_adicQuotientSystem_obj`) and the completion
  unit is universal.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.comparisonDerivedCompletionLimitAdicQuotient` — the canonical
  comparison `c`, the unique map of
  `Stacks.Algebraization.existsUnique_comparison_derivedCompletion_limit_adicQuotientSystem`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Algebraization.exists_isDerivedLimitWithProjections_single_limit_adicQuotientSystem` —
  **part (4) of [Stacks, Tag 0A0K] with its projections pinned**: the ordinary limit
  `lim ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ`, placed in degree zero, is a derived limit of the tower *through the images of the
  limit projections*. `CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.IsDerivedLimit` quantifies over the
  structural map of its triangle, so which cone the triangle carries cannot be read off part (4)
  as registered; `Stacks.Cohomology.SpaceInverseLimits.exists_distTriang_single_limit_of_isBasis`
  supplies the triangle with that map named, and `NoetherianAdicCompletion.pi_lift_comp_π`
  rewrites it as the product of its own components.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.derivedCompletionUnit_comp_completionToAdicQuotient` and
  `Stacks.Algebraization.completionToAdicQuotient_unique` — the characterization of the canonical
  maps onto the terms.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.completionToAdicQuotient_succ_comp_map` — they form a cone on the tower.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.comparison_comp_single_limit_π` — `c` followed by the `n`-th limit
  projection is the `n`-th canonical map.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.isIso_comparison_of_isDerivedLimitWithProjections` — **the reduction of
  part (3) of [Stacks, Tag 0A0K], at the tag's own hypotheses, to a single statement**: if the
  canonical maps `ℱ^∧ ⟶ (ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]` are the projections of *some* derived-limit presentation of
  `ℱ^∧`, then `c` is an isomorphism, which is part (3) read through its canonical comparison map.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.isDerivedLimitWithProjections_completionToAdicQuotient_of_isIso` — the
  converse, so that the hypothesis of the reduction is *equivalent* to part (3) rather than
  weaker than it.

## How part (3) at the tag's own hypotheses differs from the compact route

Three things make part (3) at the tag's own hypotheses different from the registered
`Stacks.Algebraization.nonempty_iso_completion_limit_adicQuotientSystem` of
`Completion/KoszulAdicCompletionLimit.lean`, which proves it on a quasi-compact scheme for an
ideal sheaf generated by global sections: that route produces an abstract isomorphism rather than
the invertibility of a map, it uses quasi-compactness for the Artin–Rees level behind the
pro-vanishing of the Koszul cohomology sheaves, and it asks for a global generating family. The
first of the three is the one with content, and the reduction above isolates it.

Spelling out the hypothesis of
`Stacks.Algebraization.isIso_comparison_of_isDerivedLimitWithProjections`: the terms `ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ` in
degree zero are derived complete, so a map `ℱ^∧ ⟶ (ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]` is determined by its restriction
along the completion unit, and the hypothesis therefore says exactly that *some* derived-limit
presentation of `ℱ^∧` over the adic quotient tower has `η ≫ πₙ = ℱ ⟶ ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ` the quotient map.
That computation is **done**, on the Koszul tower it is naturally done on.
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.completionUnit_app_comp_koszulTopSingleProjection_eq` of
`DerivedCompletionRinged/KoszulAdicDualDegreeZero.lean` computes `η ≫ πₙ` for the pinned
presentation of `ℱ^∧` over the tower of **top Koszul cohomology sheaves**
`n ↦ ℱ/(f_1^{n+1}, …, f_r^{n+1})ℱ`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedLimitWithProjections_koszulTopSingle_completion`): it is
`(-1)^r` times the quotient map `ℱ ⟶ ℱ/(f_1^{n+1}, …, f_r^{n+1})ℱ` in degree zero, the sign being
the dual twist `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.dualSign_univ` of the Koszul self-duality at the
unique top-degree factor. The same computation at the coefficient object `𝒪` is
`RingedSite.derivedStructureSheafHomIso_inv_comp_map_comp_derivedSheafHomToAdicQuotientTower`,
which is what makes part (1) of the tag available with its projections named as the canonical
factorizations of [Stacks, Tag 0CQH]
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedLimitWithProjections_adicReductionTower_completion`).

So what separates that from the hypothesis of
`Stacks.Algebraization.isIso_comparison_of_isDerivedLimitWithProjections` is no longer a cone
computation; it is the passage from the Koszul top-cohomology tower to the adic quotient tower,
and the localization. Both are now built; see the section "Part (3) is proved and registered"
below.

Two things are worth recording about why no formal manoeuvre replaces that computation. First, two
derived-limit presentations of one tower with *matching* projections do not by themselves make a
comparison invertible: the third square of the morphism of Milnor triangles is not determined,
and a counterexample is the Milnor difference map being zero, where the fibre is `P ⊕ P⟦-1⟧` and
the projection `P ⊕ P⟦-1⟧ ⟶ P` is matched by non-invertible endomorphisms. What *does* work is
the pair of lifts used in the reduction above, and it needs the projections to be the canonical
ones. Second, an abstract isomorphism `e : ℱ^∧ ≅ (lim ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]` cannot be upgraded: `e⁻¹ ≫ c` is
an endomorphism of the target which the universal property pins uniquely and gives no reason to
invert, and the same obstruction reappears after passing to `H⁰` or to affine sections, because
the identification of `H⁰(ℱ^∧)` with `lim ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ` that an abstract isomorphism provides is not
known to be compatible with the completion unit — which is the missing computation again.

## Why the canonical comparison is the right target: quasi-compactness is local, an abstract
## isomorphism is not

The pro-vanishing of the Koszul cohomology sheaves below the top degree genuinely fails on a
scheme that is not quasi-compact, and not for want of a proof: at `r = 1`, `f = x`, `ℱ = 𝒪` on the
disjoint union of the `Spec k[x]/(xⁱ)`, the level needed to kill `H⁰` on the `i`-th chart is
`n + i`, so no single level works for all charts. The site-level input
`Stacks.KoszulProZero.exists_koszulPowerComplexOnDown_homologyMap_eq_zero` carries no compactness
and asks instead for one flat Noetherian model uniform over a basis, which is exactly what a
*finite* affine cover provides and an infinite one does not.

So the tag's own hypotheses are reached by localizing, and that is the reason the canonical
comparison `c` rather than an abstract isomorphism is the object to produce: `IsIso c` descends
along a cover, where `Nonempty (ℱ^∧ ≅ (lim ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0])` does not. The descent itself is available
— `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isIso_of_coversTop_mapDerivedCategory` of
`CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/Slice/DerivedIsomorphismLocality.lean` inverts a morphism of
`D(𝒪)` whose slice restrictions over a `CoversTop` family are invertible, the affine opens of a
scheme are such a family (`Stacks.Algebraization.coversTop_affineOpens` of
`Completion/IdealSheafSiteFiniteType.lean`), and so are the two identifications a descent of `c`
needs: `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionRestrictIso` with
`derivedCompletionUnit_app_comp_derivedCompletionRestrictIso_hom_app` ([Stacks, Tag 0A0F],
`DerivedCompletionRinged/LocalizationCompletionFiniteType.lean`) for the source, and the
right-adjointness of slice restriction on `D(𝒪)`
(`Stacks.Cohomology.Localization.derivedExtensionByZeroRestrictionAdjunction`, [Stacks, Tag
08FJ]) for the limit in the target.

No passage between the slice of the opens site over an open `U` and the open subscheme `U` is
needed for this, and earlier prose here saying the descent waited on one — on a transport of the
reflector of [Stacks, Tag 099F], of `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData` or of
`Stacks.Algebraization.adicQuotientSystem` across an equivalence of ringed sites that does not
exist — was wrong about where the obstruction is. The Koszul chain is **already** available on
the slice, at the ringed-site level and with the pro-vanishing as a hypothesis rather than
`[CompactSpace Y]`: `RingedSite.isDerivedLimitWithProjections_koszulTopSingle_completion`
of `DerivedCompletionRinged/KoszulTopSingleProjections.lean`, whose hypothesis the slice
discharges by `Stacks.KoszulProZero.exists_koszulDualSystemOn_over_homologyMap_eq_zero_of_section`
at an arbitrary quasi-coherent ideal. How the descent is discharged is set out below.

## The route the missing computation can take

The adic-quotient tower is not where the canonical cone of a Koszul presentation is easiest to
compute; the *derived tensor* tower is. On any ringed site with the reflector,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.adicQuotientReduction` of
`DerivedCompletionRinged/AdicQuotientReduction.lean` is the canonical map
`K ⟶ K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪/𝓘ⁿ` of [Stacks, Tag 0CQH] and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionAdicFactor` of
`DerivedCompletionRinged/AdicQuotientFactorization.lean` is its factorization
`K^∧ ⟶ K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪/𝓘ⁿ` through the completion, *with* the compatibility with the unit
(`derivedCompletionUnit_comp_derivedCompletionAdicFactor`) and the cone law
(`derivedCompletionAdicFactor_comp_map`). Two steps separate that from the hypothesis above.

* Part (1) of the tag — `K^∧ = R lim (K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪/𝓘ⁿ)` — with *those* factorizations as the
  projections. `Stacks.Algebraization.isDerivedLimit_derivedTensor_adicQuotientSystem_completion`
  of `Completion/KoszulAdicDerivedTensorLimit.lean` proves the predicate and pins no cone;
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_derivedCompletionComparison` comes closest, but its limit
  object is existentially bound. Pinning it means carrying the structural map through
  `CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.isDerivedLimit_of_isHomotopyColimit`, whose projections are
  `H(ιₙ ≫ p)` for the structural map `p` of the homotopy colimit — so
  `CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.IsHomotopyColimit` has to be refined to name `p`. That
  refinement is [Stacks, Tag 0CRH] and it **is** formalized:
  `CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.IsHomotopyColimitWithCoprojections` of
  `CategoryTheory/Triangulated/HomotopyColimitCoprojections.lean`, with the tag's uniqueness
  statement as `CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.exists_iso_of_isHomotopyColimitWithCoprojections`.
  The form that carries it into a derived limit is
  `CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.exists_distTriang_pi_lift_map_coprojections`, and its
  instances at the derived internal Hom are
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedLimitWithProjections_derivedSheafHom_of_coprojections`
  and `RingedSite.isDerivedLimitWithProjections_tensorDerivedDual_of_coprojections`
  of `DerivedCompletionRinged/DerivedHomHomotopyColimitProjections.lean` — [Stacks, Tag 0A0A]
  with its projections pinned at `R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(iₙ, E)`.
* `NoetherianAdicCompletion.IsDerivedLimitWithProjections.comp_of_hasEventualInverses` then
  carries the projections along a *natural transformation* of towers with eventual inverses, and
  the truncation `K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪/𝓘ⁿ ⟶ (ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]` is natural — unlike the pro-isomorphisms of the
  Koszul chain, which reindex. Its `NoetherianAdicCompletion.HasEventualInverses` is the
  pro-vanishing of the higher Tor sheaves, that is Artin–Rees, and is where quasi-compactness of
  the ambient scheme genuinely enters; on an affine open it is available.

## The localization, which is how part (3) is obtained

One thing stood between the pinned Koszul presentation and part (3), and it is not a cone
computation.

* **The localization.** The pro-vanishing hypothesis of the pinned presentation is false on a
  scheme that is not quasi-compact (see above), so the presentation can only be had on the slices,
  and the invertibility of `c` has to be descended along them. That descent is
  `Stacks.Algebraization.isIso_comparison_of_affineOpens` of
  `Completion/AdicQuotientSliceDescent.lean`: its hypothesis is a derived-limit presentation of
  `(ℱ|_V)^∧` over the **restricted** adic quotient tower whose projections are the restrictions
  of the canonical maps `ℱ^∧ ⟶ (ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ)[0]`, for `V` ranging over the affine opens. The three
  inputs it needed are 0A0F for the source
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionRestrictIso`, with its compatibility with the
  unit), the preservation of the countable products of `D(𝒪)` by slice restriction for the limit
  in the target
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.preservesProductsOfShape_nat_overFunctor_mapDerivedCategory`,
  [Stacks, Tag 08FJ]), and the transport of a derived-limit presentation *with its projections*
  along a triangulated functor
  (`NoetherianAdicCompletion.IsDerivedLimitWithProjections.map`).

The pigeonhole comparison of the two quotient towers, which used to be the other missing piece,
is available: the pinned presentation above is over
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.koszulTopHomologySystem`, whose `n`-th term is
`ℱ/(f_1^{n+1}, …, f_r^{n+1})ℱ`, while part (3) is over `n ↦ ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ`, and the containments
`𝓘^{r(n+1)+1}ℱ ⊆ (f_1^{n+1}, …, f_r^{n+1})ℱ ⊆ 𝓘ⁿℱ` turn the one into the other with eventual
inverses, using nothing Noetherian, compact or coherent. At ringed-site level that is
`RingedSite.exists_hasEventualInverses_koszulTopHomologySystem_adicQuotientTowerOn`,
over the tower `Stacks.Sites.adicQuotientTowerOn` of
`CategoryTheory/Sites/Modules/SubmoduleQuotientTower.lean`, at an arbitrary coefficient and with
no hypothesis at all; on a **scheme** it is
`Stacks.Algebraization.koszulTopQuotientToAdicQuotient` with
`hasEventualInverses_koszulTopQuotientToAdicQuotient` of
`DerivedCompletionRinged/KoszulTopQuotientAdicProIso.lean`. Since a site-level `ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ` now exists,
the localization can use the slice's own tower rather than the restriction of the scheme's, and the
comparison of the two is `Stacks.Sites.adicQuotientTowerOnOffset` of
`DerivedCompletionRinged/KoszulAdicQuotientTowerOffset.lean`.

### Part (3)

Part (3) needs three pieces — the offset of the two
site-level adic quotient towers, the identification of the Čech completion with the reflector
carrying its unit equation, and the transport of the pinning through the two. **All three are
built, and part (3) at the tag's own hypotheses is registered** as
`Stacks.Algebraization.isIso_comparisonDerivedCompletionLimitAdicQuotient` of
`Completion/AdicQuotientSliceTowerKoszul.lean`: for a coherent `ℱ` on a locally Noetherian `X`,
not assumed quasi-compact, and an arbitrary quasi-coherent `𝓘`, the canonical comparison `c` is
an isomorphism.

The route is the one the reduction above isolates. `Stacks.Algebraization`
`.isIso_comparison_of_affineOpens_sliceTower` of `Completion/AdicQuotientSliceTowerDescent.lean`
reduces `IsIso c` to a derived-limit presentation of `(ℱ|_V)^∧` over the slice's own adic
quotient tower through the slice's own canonical maps, for `V` affine; and
`Stacks.Algebraization.exists_isDerivedLimitWithProjections_completionToAdicQuotientSliceTower`
supplies that from the Koszul chain, by

1. `Stacks.Sites.adicQuotientTowerOnOffset` of
   `DerivedCompletionRinged/KoszulAdicQuotientTowerOffset.lean`, the interleaving
   `ℱ/𝓘ⁿ⁺¹ℱ ⟶ ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ` induced by `𝓘ⁿ⁺¹ ⊆ 𝓘ⁿ`, with eventual inverses one level up — the terms of
   `Stacks.Sites.adicQuotientTowerOn` and `Stacks.Sites.idealSmulQuotientTower` coincide by `rfl`
   one index apart, but the towers are not isomorphic as `ℕᵒᵖ`-functors, `idealPow_zero` giving
   `𝓘⁰ = ⊤`;
2. `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_iso_derivedCompletion_completionFunctor_obj`, whose
   statement now carries `derivedCompletionUnit ≫ θ.hom = completionUnit` rather than being a
   bare `Nonempty` — which is what moves a *pinned* presentation between the two models of the
   completion;
3. `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.completionUnit_app_comp_koszulTopSingleProjection_comp_map_eq`,
   the cone of the transported Koszul projections, in which the `(-1)^r` of the Koszul
   self-duality cancels against the sign of the twisted pigeonhole comparison.

The pigeonhole comparison at an arbitrary coefficient is not among the pieces — it is available
over `adicQuotientTowerOn`, as `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite`
`.exists_hasEventualInverses_koszulTopHomologySystem_adicQuotientTowerOn` — but it binds the
comparison existentially, so a *named* coefficient-ful form with a cone identity is given:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.koszulTopHomologyToIdealSmulQuotientTower`.

## Implementation notes

Two spellings of the category of `𝒪_X`-modules meet here. `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules` is a
`def` for `SheafOfModules X.ringCatSheaf`, while the derived-completeness predicate and the
reflector of [Stacks, Tag 099F] are stated for `SheafOfModules R`, where `R` is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite X.sheaf).O`. The two
sheaves of rings are definitionally but not syntactically equal, so — as in
`Completion/AdicQuotientLimitComplete.lean` — each statement ascribes its objects into one spelling
and the crossings are confined to `show … from` at the statement boundary. Part (4) is stated at
the `X.Modules` spelling, because that is the spelling of the basis-of-affine-opens input it is
proved from, and the reduction crosses once, by `exact`, which unifies at default transparency
where `rw` does not.

`CategoryTheory.Limits.Pi.lift` takes the indexed family as an implicit argument, and elaborating
it from the components alone produces `fun n ↦ G.obj (F.obj ⟨n⟩)` where the goal carries
`fun n ↦ (F ⋙ G).obj ⟨n⟩`. The two are definitionally equal, so `exact` crosses them, but a
`rw` whose pattern is spelled the first way silently fails to match a goal spelled the second
way — the family is therefore given explicitly with `(f := …)` wherever a rewrite is involved.
The `HasProduct` arguments are irrelevant to this, `HasLimit` being a `Prop`.

